  "strategy": "### Approach and rationale\n\nThe problem of minimizing the overlap integral C\u2085 for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem is approached using a mixed-integer convex programming (MICP) framework. This strategy combines discrete decisions about step positions with continuous optimization of step heights, allowing for a structured exploration of the solution space. The method leverages the initial_h_values as a warm start to accelerate convergence and ensures feasibility through explicit constraint handling.\n\n### Mathematical specification\n\nDefine the step function h: [0, 2] \u2192 [0, 1] as a collection of intervals where h is constant. Let positions p\u2081, p\u2082, ..., p\u2099 indicate the start of each step, and heights h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h\u2099 represent their respective values. The width of each step is \u0394x, determined by the discretization dx = 2.0 / n_points. The objective is:\n$$\n\\text{minimize} \\quad C_5 = \\max_k \\sum_{i=1}^{n\\_points} h[i](1 - h[i + k]) \\cdot dx\n$$\nsubject to:\n$$\n0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^{n\\_points} h[i] \\cdot dx = 1\n$$\nThe MICP formulation represents step positions as integer variables and heights as continuous variables, enabling a precise balance between computational exploration and efficiency.\n\n### Implementation procedure\n\n1. **Initialization**:\n   - Convert initial_h_values to step positions and heights.\n   - Define MICP variables: integer positions p and continuous heights h.\n\n2. **Formulate MICP problem**:\n   - Express the overlap integrals using convolution for all shifts k.\n   - Formulate the sum constraints and bounds on h.\n\n3. **Solve MICP**:\n   - Use a solver (e.g., Gurobi) to minimize the maximum overlap integral.\n   - Warm-start the solver with initial_h_values to accelerate convergence.\n\n4. **Iterative refinement**:\n   - If the solver plateaus, introduce new variables to refine step widths.\n   - Adjust solver parameters (e.g., optimality gap) to balance speed and accuracy.\n\n5. **Termination and validation**:\n   - Return the best solution when the time budget is exhausted.\n   - Validate the solution ensures h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] and the sum constraint.\n\n### Search choices and variations\n\n- **Solver selection**: Use Gurobi for MICP due to its handling of mixed-integer problems.\n- **Warm-start initialization**: Start with initial_h_values to exploit prior information.\n- **Problem size**: Begin with smaller step counts and incrementally increase for refinement.\n\n### Compute budget and robustness\n\n- **Time allocation**: 80% for solving, 20% for validation and refinement.\n- **Progress monitoring**: Check solver progress every 100 iterations to adapt parameters.\n\n### Validation and return contract\n\n- Ensure h satisfies constraints and re-evaluate C\u2085 to confirm feasibility.",
